@.str.186 = private unnamed_addr constant [2080 x i8] c"Creates an UV atlas and adds it as triangle attr 'texture_uvs' to the mesh.\0A\0AInput meshes must be manifold for this method to work.\0AThe algorithm is based on:\0AZhou et al, \22Iso-charts: Stretch-driven Mesh Parameterization using Spectral Analysis\22, Eurographics Symposium on Geometry Processing (2004)\0ASander et al. \22Signal-Specialized Parametrization\22 Europgraphics 2002\0AThis function always uses the CPU device.\0A\0AArgs:\0A    size (int): The target size of the texture (size x size). The uv coordinates\0A        will still be in the range [0..1] but parameters like gutter use pixels\0A        as units.\0A    gutter (float): This is the space around the uv islands in pixels.\0A    max_stretch (float): The maximum amount of stretching allowed. The parameter\0A        range is [0..1] with 0 meaning no stretch allowed.\0A\0A    parallel_partitions (int): The approximate number of partitions created\0A        before computing the UV atlas for parallelizing the computation.\0A        Parallelization can be enabled with values > 1. Note that\0A        parallelization increases the number of UV islands and can lead to results\0A        with lower quality.\0A\0A    nthreads (int): The number of threads used when parallel_partitions\0A        is > 1. Set to 0 for automatic number of thread detection.\0A\0AReturns:\0A    This function creates a face attribute \22texture_uvs\22 and returns a tuple\0A    with (max stretch, num_charts, num_partitions) storing the\0A    actual amount of stretch, the number of created charts, and the number of\0A    parallel partitions created.\0A\0AExample:\0A    This code creates a uv map for the Stanford Bunny mesh::\0A\0A        import open3d as o3d\0A        bunny = o3d.data.BunnyMesh()\0A        mesh = o3d.t.geometry.TriangleMesh.from_legacy(o3d.io.read_triangle_mesh(bunny.path))\0A        mesh.compute_uvatlas()\0A\0A        # Add a wood texture and visualize\0A        texture_data = o3d.data.WoodTexture()\0A        mesh.material.material_name = 'defaultLit'\0A        mesh.material.texture_maps['albedo'] = o3d.t.io.read_image(texture_data.albedo_texture_path)\0A        o3d.visualization.draw(mesh)\0A\00", align 1
